[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: web_server_0
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not DNS or general reachability. From host_1_1_1_1, web0.local resolves to 10.200.0.3 (getent hosts). HTTP works and returns 200 OK quickly (curl http://web0.local and http://10.200.0.3 both succeed). The failure is specific to HTTPS: curl -k https://web0.local and https://10.200.0.3 return 000 immediately, indicating no TLS service. On web_server_0, Apache is running and listening only on 0.0.0.0:80 (ss/netstat), and there is no listener on 443. Apache’s config shows only a :80 VirtualHost (apachectl -S) and no SSL modules loaded (apachectl -M shows neither ssl nor gnutls). Although ports.conf contains conditional “Listen 443,” it is inactive because the SSL module is not enabled.

Mechanism: clients attempting https://web0.local (e.g., via bookmark, HTTPS-First/HSTS) try TCP/443 to 10.200.0.3, which is closed, causing connection failures/timeouts. Hence the browser “spins,” while plain HTTP works.

Localization: Apache HTTP Server on web_server_0, TLS/HTTPS configuration (module and vhost) missing.

Root cause: HTTPS was inadvertently disabled/misconfigured on web_server_0 (no SSL module/vhost), leaving port 443 closed.

Corrective fix: Re-enable HTTPS on web_server_0. For Debian/Apache:
- a2enmod ssl
- a2ensite default-ssl (or create a proper <VirtualHost *:443> for web0.local with SSLCertificateFile/KeyFile)
- systemctl restart apache2
Verify ss shows LISTEN on :443 and curl -k https://web0.local returns 200. As a temporary workaround, users can use http://web0.local until TLS is restored. 
=== END ===